{s:2,k:'notebook',term:'Key ideas',title:'The three efficiency formulas',pause:1.4,say:'The Otto efficiency depends only on the compression ratio and gamma. Push the ratio past about ten to twelve, and a petrol engine knocks. Diesel cut-off at a fraction k of the stroke gives one plus k times the ratio minus one. And the Dual formula holds both as limits.',cues:{i1:'The Otto efficiency',i2:'Push the ratio',i3:'Diesel cut-off at',i4:'And the Dual'},items:[[`Otto: η = 1 − 1/r${SP('γ−1')}, set by r and γ only`,'i1'],['Petrol knocks above r ≈ 10 to 12','i2'],[`Diesel: r${SB('c')} = 1 + k(r − 1)`,'i3'],[`Dual: r${SB('c')} = 1 gives Otto, r${SB('p')} = 1 gives Diesel`,'i4']],vis:(c,u,b)=>{
 const ax=80,ay=300,rx=r=>ax+(r-2)/12*500,ey=e=>ay-e/.7*236;
 const curve=MT_range(2,14,48).map(r=>[rx(r),ey(1-Math.pow(r,-.4))]);
 let ticks='';[4,8,12].forEach(r=>{ticks+=MT_L(rx(r),ay,rx(r),ay+8,MT_DARK,2)+MT_T(rx(r),ay+32,String(r),{fs:22,a:'middle',fill:MT_GREY})});
 [[.3,'30 %'],[.6,'60 %']].forEach(([e,t])=>{ticks+=MT_L(ax-8,ey(e),ax,ey(e),MT_DARK,2)+MT_T(ax-12,ey(e)+8,t,{fs:22,a:'end',fill:MT_GREY})});
 const p1=MT_T(20,36,'Otto: η rises with r',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:BLU})+MT_axes(ax,ay,520,250,'r','η')+ticks+MT_poly(curve,BLU,5)+
  `<circle cx="${rx(8)}" cy="${ey(1-Math.pow(8,-.4))}" r="9" fill="${BLU}"/>`+MT_T(rx(8)-10,ey(1-Math.pow(8,-.4))+42,'r = 8: 56.5 %',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU});
 const p2=`<rect x="${rx(10)}" y="${ey(.7)}" width="${rx(12)-rx(10)}" height="${ay-ey(.7)}" fill="${RED}" fill-opacity=".14"/><rect x="${rx(12)}" y="${ey(.7)}" width="${rx(14)-rx(12)}" height="${ay-ey(.7)}" fill="${RED}" fill-opacity=".3"/>`+
  MT_T(rx(12),ey(.3),'knock',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:RED})+MT_T(rx(12),ey(.3)+30,'r ≈ 10 to 12',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:RED});
 const bx=40,bw=540,ux=v=>bx+v/15*bw,by=414;
 const p3=MT_T(20,388,'Diesel cut-off: r = 15, k = 0.06',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:ORG})+
  MT_box(bx,by,bw,52,{fill:'#FFFFFF',st:MT_DARK,sw:3,r:4})+`<rect x="${bx}" y="${by}" width="${ux(1)-bx}" height="52" fill="${MT_LIGHT}"/><rect x="${ux(1)}" y="${by}" width="${ux(1.84)-ux(1)}" height="52" fill="${ORG}" fill-opacity=".8"/>`+
  MT_T(ux(.5),by+80,'v₂',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:MT_GREY})+MT_T(ux(1.84)+4,by+80,'v₃',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:ORG})+MT_T(ux(15),by+80,'v₁',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'end',fill:MT_GREY})+
  MT_T(ux(2.4),by+36,`r${SB('c')} = 1 + 0.06 × 14 = 1.84`,{fs:24,fw:800,fill:ORG});
 const p4=MT_T(20,556,'Dual holds both',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:GRN})+MT_box(30,576,130,104,{fill:'#EBFBEE',st:GRN,sw:3,r:12})+MT_T(95,638,'Dual',{fs:28,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN})+
  MT_arrow(164,602,222,602,GRN,4,14)+MT_arrow(164,654,222,654,GRN,4,14)+
  MT_box(230,578,380,48,{fill:'#FFFFFF',st:BLU,sw:3,r:10})+MT_T(250,611,`r${SB('c')} = 1: the Otto formula`,{fs:24,fw:800,fill:BLU})+
  MT_box(230,630,380,48,{fill:'#FFFFFF',st:ORG,sw:3,r:10})+MT_T(250,663,`r${SB('p')} = 1: the Diesel formula`,{fs:24,fw:800,fill:ORG});
 return g('pop',tk(c,'i1'),p1)+g('pop',tk(c,'i2'),p2)+g('pop',tk(c,'i3'),p3)+g('pop',tk(c,'i4'),p4)}},
/* book Worked example (Otto), 6 steps over two calc scenes */
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 2",title:"Worked example 2: an Otto cycle",say:'Eight to the power point four is two point two nine seven. So T two is six hundred eighty-nine point two kelvin, at eighteen point three eight bar. The volumes follow from R T over p. Then eight hundred kilojoules at constant volume lift T three to eighteen hundred three point four kelvin.',cues:{fk:'Eight to the',k1:'to the power',k1b:'So T two',k2:'The volumes follow',k2b:'R T over',k3:'Then eight hundred',k3b:'lift T three'},q:[`Otto cycle, r = 8; air at 1 bar and 300 K; q${SB('in')} = 800 kJ/kg.`,`c${SB('v')} = 0.718, R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K, γ = 1.4. Find T and p at each state, η and w${SB('net')}.`],f:[`T₂ = T₁r${SP('γ−1')}, p₂ = p₁r${SP('γ')}`,`T₃ = T₂ + q${SB('in')}/c${SB('v')}`,`η = 1 − 1/r${SP('γ−1')}`],hint:'r = v₁/v₂; temperatures in kelvin.',fk:'fk',steps:MT_OS,live:[0,1,2],rh:82},
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 2 (efficiency)",title:"Worked example 2: an Otto cycle",say:'Expansion divides by two point two nine seven again: seven hundred eighty-five kelvin, at two point six two bar. Heat out is three hundred forty-eight point two, leaving four hundred fifty-one point eight of net work. Efficiency: fifty-six point five percent, as the formula says.',pause:1,cues:{fk:'Expansion divides by',k4:'divides by two',k4b:'seven hundred eighty-five',k5:'Heat out is',k5b:'leaving four hundred',k6:'Efficiency',k6b:'fifty-six point five',a:'as the formula'},q:[`Otto cycle, r = 8; air at 1 bar and 300 K; q${SB('in')} = 800 kJ/kg.`,`c${SB('v')} = 0.718, R = 0.287 kJ/kg·K, γ = 1.4. Find T and p at each state, η and w${SB('net')}.`],f:[`T₂ = T₁r${SP('γ−1')}, p₂ = p₁r${SP('γ')}`,`T₃ = T₂ + q${SB('in')}/c${SB('v')}`,`η = 1 − 1/r${SP('γ−1')}`],hint:'Expansion uses the same 2.297.',fk:'fk',steps:MT_OS,live:[3,4,5],rh:82,ans:[`η = 56.5 %, w${SB('net')} = 451.8 kJ/kg`,'a']},
/* book Worked example 2 (Diesel), 6 steps over two calc scenes */
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 3",title:"Worked example 3: a Diesel cycle",say:'Cut-off at six percent of the stroke gives one plus point zero six times fourteen: one point eight four. Compression gives eight hundred eighty-six point three kelvin. Constant-pressure heat multiplies that by the same ratio: one thousand six hundred thirty point seven.',cues:{fk:'Cut-off at six',k1:'gives one plus',k1b:'one point eight',k2:'Compression gives',k2b:'eight hundred eighty-six',k3:'Constant-pressure heat',k3b:'one thousand six'},q:['Diesel cycle, r = 15; cut-off at 6 % of the stroke; air at 1 bar and 300 K.',`c${SB('p')} = 1.005, c${SB('v')} = 0.718 kJ/kg·K, γ = 1.4. Find r${SB('c')}, the temperatures, q${SB('in')}, q${SB('out')} and η.`],f:[`r${SB('c')} = 1 + k(r − 1)`,`T₃ = r${SB('c')}T₂, T₄ = T₁r${SB('c')}${SP('γ')}`,`q${SB('in')} = c${SB('p')}ΔT, q${SB('out')} = c${SB('v')}ΔT`],hint:'k = 0.06, the fraction of the stroke.',fk:'fk',steps:MT_DS,live:[0,1,2],rh:82},
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 3 (efficiency)",title:"Worked example 3: a Diesel cycle",say:'Expansion ends at seven hundred four point five kelvin. Heat in uses c p: seven hundred forty-eight point two. Heat out uses c v: two hundred ninety point four. So the efficiency is sixty-one point two percent.',pause:1,cues:{fk:'Expansion ends at',k4:'ends at seven',k4b:'four point five',k5:'Heat in uses',k5b:'Heat out uses',k6:'So the efficiency',k6b:'sixty-one point',a:'point two percent'},q:['Diesel cycle, r = 15; cut-off at 6 % of the stroke; air at 1 bar and 300 K.',`c${SB('p')} = 1.005, c${SB('v')} = 0.718 kJ/kg·K, γ = 1.4. Find r${SB('c')}, the temperatures, q${SB('in')}, q${SB('out')} and η.`],f:[`r${SB('c')} = 1 + k(r − 1)`,`T₃ = r${SB('c')}T₂, T₄ = T₁r${SB('c')}${SP('γ')}`,`q${SB('in')} = c${SB('p')}ΔT, q${SB('out')} = c${SB('v')}ΔT`],hint:`c${SB('p')} at constant p, c${SB('v')} at constant v.`,fk:'fk',steps:MT_DS,live:[3,4,5],rh:82,ans:[`r${SB('c')} = 1.84, η = 61.2 %`,'a']},
{s:2,k:'trap2',term:'Exam trap',title:'Exam trap!',say:'Exam trap. Never use c v for the Diesel heat input: the heat goes in at constant pressure, and the answer comes out twenty-nine percent short. Write c p for the heat in, c v for the heat out.',cues:{x:'Exam trap',w:'Never use',r:'Write c p'},wl:"Don't write",rl:'Write',wrong:[`Diesel: q${SB('in')} = c${SB('v')}(T₃ − T₂)`,'= 534.5 kJ/kg'],right:[`q${SB('in')} = c${SB('p')}(T₃ − T₂) = 748.2 kJ/kg`,`q${SB('out')} = c${SB('v')}(T₄ − T₁) = 290.4 kJ/kg`],wv:(c,u,b)=>{const D=MT_dies(60,290,280,250);
 return MT_axes(60,290,300,262,'v','p')+MT_T(200,322,'sketch: v on a log scale',{fs:20,a:'middle',fill:MT_GREY})+MT_poly(D.comp,MT_GREY,4)+MT_poly(D.exp,MT_GREY,4)+MT_L(D.px(D.v1),D.py(D.p4),D.px(D.v1),D.py(1),MT_GREY,4)+
  MT_L(D.px(D.v2),D.py(D.p2),D.px(D.v3),D.py(D.p2),TRED,8)+MT_T(D.px(D.v3)+14,D.py(D.p2)+8,`c${SB('v')} ?`,{fs:28,fw:800,fill:TRED})+
  MT_T(400,150,'534.5 kJ/kg',{fs:30,fw:800,fill:TRED})+MT_T(400,188,'29 % too small',{fs:22,fill:MT_GREY})},
 rv:(c,u,b)=>{const D=MT_dies(60,290,280,250);
 return MT_axes(60,290,300,262,'v','p')+MT_T(200,322,'sketch: v on a log scale',{fs:20,a:'middle',fill:MT_GREY})+MT_poly(D.comp,MT_GREY,4)+MT_poly(D.exp,MT_GREY,4)+MT_L(D.px(D.v1),D.py(D.p4),D.px(D.v1),D.py(1),BLU,8)+
  MT_L(D.px(D.v2),D.py(D.p2),D.px(D.v3),D.py(D.p2),GRN,8)+MT_T(D.px(D.v3)+14,D.py(D.p2)+8,`c${SB('p')}: constant p`,{fs:26,fw:800,fill:GRN})+
  MT_T(D.px(D.v1)+16,D.py(D.p4)+4,`c${SB('v')}: constant v`,{fs:26,fw:800,fill:BLU})+MT_T(420,120,'heat in',{fs:22,fill:MT_GREY})+MT_T(420,154,'748.2 kJ/kg',{fs:28,fw:800,fill:GRN})}},
/* ---- recap ---- */
{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:'Air standard cycles: fixed air, reversible steps, constant specific heats, Carnot the limit. Otto adds heat at constant volume, Diesel at constant pressure, Dual both. And two rules: kelvin, and c p only at constant pressure.',cues:{r1:'Air standard cycles',r2:'Otto adds heat',r3:'And two rules'},panels:[["Air standard cycles","BLU",'mtCarnotIc',[`Fixed air, reversible, constant c${SB('p')}, c${SB('v')}.`,`Carnot limit: η = 1 − T${SB('L')}/T${SB('H')}`],'r1'],["Otto, Diesel, Dual","ORG",'mtThreeIc',['Heat in: constant v, constant p,','or both; rejected at constant v.'],'r2'],["Two exam rules","GRN",'mtRulesIc',['Temperatures in kelvin.',`c${SB('p')} at constant p, c${SB('v')} at constant v.`],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Give two reasons why the Carnot cycle is not used for a real engine.",say:'Question one. Give two reasons why the Carnot cycle is not used for a real engine.',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Give two reasons'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Give two reasons why the Carnot cycle is not used for a real engine.",say:'First, isothermal heat transfer needs very slow processes, impossible at engine speeds. Second, huge pressure and volume ratios give a small net work: a low work ratio and mean effective pressure, so friction eats the output.',cues:{a1:'First, isothermal heat',a2:'Second, huge pressure'},al:[["Isothermal heat transfer needs very slow processes: impossible at engine speeds.",'a1'],["Huge pressure and volume ratios for a small net work: a low work ratio and MEP, so friction eats the output.",'a2']],vis:(c,u,b)=>{
 const cx=170,cy=200,R0=120,ang=f=>Math.PI*(1-f),pt=(f,r)=>[cx+r*Math.cos(ang(f)),cy-r*Math.sin(ang(f))];
 let dial=`<path d="M${cx-R0},${cy} A${R0},${R0} 0 0 1 ${cx+R0},${cy}" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="${MT_DARK}" stroke-width="5"/>`;
 MT_range(0,1,6).forEach(f=>{const [x1,y1]=pt(f,R0-4),[x2,y2]=pt(f,R0-22);dial+=MT_L(x1,y1,x2,y2,MT_DARK,3)});
 const [nx,ny]=pt(.86,R0-30);dial+=MT_L(cx,cy,nx,ny,ORG,7)+`<circle cx="${cx}" cy="${cy}" r="10" fill="${MT_DARK}"/>`+MT_T(cx,cy+40,'engine speed',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:MT_GREY});
 const a1=dial+MT_T(330,110,'thousands of rpm',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:ORG})+MT_T(330,150,'isothermal heat',{fs:22,fill:MT_GREY})+MT_T(330,180,'needs a slow piston',{fs:22,fill:MT_GREY});
 const k=.85,a2=MT_T(40,298,'positive work',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:MT_GREY})+`<rect x="40" y="310" width="${(609.8*k).toFixed(1)}" height="46" rx="6" fill="${ORG}" fill-opacity=".85"/>`+MT_T(56,343,'609.8 kJ/kg',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:WHT})+
  MT_T(40,392,'net work',{fs:22,fw:700,fill:MT_GREY})+`<rect x="40" y="404" width="${(119.4*k).toFixed(1)}" height="46" rx="6" fill="${GRN}" fill-opacity=".9"/>`+MT_T(40+119.4*k+14,437,'119.4 kJ/kg',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:GRN})+
  MT_T(40,494,'work ratio = 0.196',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:MT_DARK});
 return g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),a1)+g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),a2)}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A Carnot engine works between 1000 K and 300 K and takes in 200 kJ/kg of heat. Find its efficiency, net work and heat rejected.",say:'Question two. A Carnot engine works between one thousand and three hundred kelvin, taking in two hundred kilojoules per kilogram. Find its efficiency, net work and heat rejected.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'A Carnot engine'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A Carnot engine works between 1000 K and 300 K and takes in 200 kJ/kg of heat. Find its efficiency, net work and heat rejected.",say:'Efficiency first: one minus three hundred over one thousand, which is seventy percent. Net work is seventy percent of two hundred: one hundred forty kilojoules per kilogram. The other sixty are rejected.',cues:{fk:'Efficiency first',k1:'one minus three',k1b:'which is seventy',k2:'Net work is',k2b:'one hundred forty',k3:'The other',k3b:'sixty are'},pause:1,calc:{f:[`η = 1 − T${SB('L')}/T${SB('H')}`,`w${SB('net')} = η × q${SB('in')}`,`q${SB('out')} = q${SB('in')} − w${SB('net')}`],hint:'Both temperatures are already in kelvin.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Efficiency",["1 − 300 / 1000","η = 0.70"],["k1","k1b"]],["Net work",["0.70 × 200",`w${SB('net')} = 140 kJ/kg`],["k2","k2b"]],["Heat rejected",["200 − 140",`q${SB('out')} = 60 kJ/kg`],["k3","k3b"]]],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"In the Carnot worked example, the work ratio is 0.196: 119.4 kJ/kg net from 609.8 kJ/kg of positive work. Suppose friction takes 10% of the positive work. What happens to the net output?",say:'Question three. In the Carnot example, the work ratio is point one nine six. If friction takes ten percent of the positive work, what happens to the net output?',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'In the Carnot'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"In the Carnot worked example, the work ratio is 0.196: 119.4 kJ/kg net from 609.8 kJ/kg of positive work. Suppose friction takes 10% of the positive work. What happens to the net output?",say:'Friction takes a tenth of six hundred nine point eight: sixty-one kilojoules per kilogram. The net output falls from one hundred nineteen point four to fifty-eight point four, about half. A low work ratio magnifies small losses.',cues:{fk:'Friction takes a',k1:'a tenth of',k1b:'sixty-one kilojoules',k2:'The net output',k2b:'about half',k3:'A low work'},calc:{f:['loss = 0.10 × positive work',`net = w${SB('net')} − loss`],hint:'Positive work is 609.8 kJ/kg.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Friction loss",["0.10 × 609.8","61.0 kJ/kg lost"],["k1","k1b"]],["Net output",["119.4 − 61.0","58.4 kJ/kg: about half"],["k2","k2b"]],["Why","Low work ratio: small losses take a big share","k3"]],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:`Your free notes are the PDF in the description. Redraw the Carnot rectangle and the three ${P('p-v')} sketches.`,cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the Carnot'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["In this lesson", "Air standard cycles and their assumptions; Otto, Diesel and Dual cycles"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["The Carnot cycle on p-v and T-s", "300 K to 900 K: a rectangle on T-s, a thin sliver on p-v that needs a pressure ratio of 94."], ["Otto, Diesel and Dual cycles", "Heat in on a vertical line (Otto), a horizontal line (Diesel) or both (Dual); all three reject heat at constant volume."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: P. K. Nag (Engineering Thermodynamics); Yunus A. Çengel and Michael A. Boles (Thermodynamics: An Engineering Approach); R. K. Rajput (Engineering Thermodynamics)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:'So remember: Carnot sets the limit, the heat path sets the cycle, and kelvin every time. Next time, Brayton and Stirling cycles. See you there!',cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'Carnot sets the',w2:'the heat path',w3:'and kelvin every',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['CARNOT SETS THE LIMIT','BLU','w1'],['HEAT PATH SETS THE CYCLE','ORG','w2'],['KELVIN, EVERY TIME','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Brayton and Stirling cycles", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
